{
  "assessment": "The source packet is the main text and supplemental material of a Physical Review Letters paper introducing a hard-disk model driven by active collisions. The paper reports three distinct non-equilibrium universality classes for liquid-gas interfaces (|q|KPZ, wet-|q|KPZ, hyperuniform) and a new class for liquid-solid and liquid-glass interfaces where the roughness exponent shifts toward zero. The extraction focuses on the liquid-gas interface universality classes, which provide the clearest quantitative relations between controls (system size L, wavenumber k) and readouts (static height correlation S_h, interface width squared W^2, saturation time tau_ss). The paper reports measured exponents and scaling relations for each class. The selected cluster is the liquid-gas interface scaling, with two regimes: the |q|KPZ class (T != 0, Gamma != 0) and the wet-|q|KPZ class (Gamma = 0). These share the same controls (L, k) and readouts (S_h, W^2, tau_ss) and are directly comparable. The hyperuniform class is also reported but is a third regime; it is included as a separate claim. The liquid-solid and liquid-glass results are qualitatively described but lack precise quantitative relations for the selected controls, so they are not included in the main claims. The paper provides sufficient quantitative data for the liquid-gas classes: S_h ~ k^{-1.5} for |q|KPZ, S_h ~ k^{-3} for wet-|q|KPZ, W^2 ~ t^{0.18} for |q|KPZ, W_ss^2 ~ L^2 for wet-|q|KPZ, and tau_ss ~ L^z with z ~ 2.78 for |q|KPZ and z ~ 3 for hyperuniform. These are encoded as claims with mechanisms and parameters. The case is authoring-ready because there are numeric controls (L, k), numeric readouts (S_h, W^2, tau_ss), quantitative mechanisms (scaling laws), and explicit domains.",
